In article <75dvi3$4c0$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>,  <lshurr@my-dejanews.com> wrote:
>I have a client running Digital Unix 3.2.  For some reason, "ls -l" displays
>numeric uid's & gid's, as though you entered "ls -n" *UNLESS* your username
>is in the system group.  In the latter case, "ls -l" looks as it should.
>What have I missed?

It sounds like one or more of the following files are misprotected:

/etc/passwd
/etc/group
/etc/svc.conf

They all should be mode 644, so that programs like ls can read them.


