Vitor Beires Nogueira wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> 
>  recently I discovered that I'm supposed to administer and
> Digital Dec  3000 with OS OSF1 and file system ADVFS.
> All went well except for the fact that the machine has crashed this
> weekend.
> 
>     I managed to spot the problem:
>     one of the SCSI disks seems to give reading  errors (cam_error).
> The big problem is that disk that disk has some volumes that belong to a 
> very important domain (domain users that contains filesets /home and
> /var/spool/mail).

Trouble. AdvFS is relatively immune to power failures, due to it's
Transaction Logging feature. But hardware failures have a tendency to
kill it.

>     Is there a way to bypass those volumes and losing only some
> information ? (I
> lost the remvol command also) ? This is probably the last thing I should
> try, right ?

rmvol is supposed to work on a non damaged hardware. Even if you get it
somehow and do a rmvol it is very likely to crash again.

> 
>     I tried to copy that problematic disk into another (with identical
> geometry) using Linux dd but that
> doesn't seem to work either because Linux get some reading errors.
>     Is there any other way to copy that disk in a way that it can
> recover more info ?

A read failure is a read failure. No "miracle program" can assist you on
that situation. You could try to plug that new disk in anyway. It is
working, no read errors, so it could possibly mount. And then the best
thing to go for is "vdump". Backup that FS, then you could try restoring
it to yet another disk and if that goes OK, you have a functioning File
System. If there are errors while backing up, try to ignore them.

Oh, and BTW, have you checked the cables? I've seen weird thins
happening, like the cable falling into the central unit cooling fan on
AS 2100.

Nix.

-- 
