On Wed, 9 Dec 1998 ivan.dolezal@vsb.cz wrote:

> I got a really dumb question: if I create a soft link, its permission is
> lrwxrwxrwx .
> 
> How can I change it?

You can't. Links don't have any permissions, only files and other things
like devices and pipes which are represented by inodes, have. 
An inode is a data structure containing vital data describing the file,
including permissions, owner, ...

When you use a link, the permissions of the file pointed to will apply.
While ls will display the permissions of the file associated with a hard
link, it will always show lrwxrwxrwx for soft links.

Simple experiment: If a file has more than one hard link, ls will display
identical permissions (as well as ownership, access time, ...) for all
of them.

-- 

