On Wed, 9 Dec 1998 ivan.dolezal@vsb.cz wrote:

> Thanks for your asset, but I can see in my Digital Unix's root
> 
> lrwxr-xr-x   1 root     system          7 Oct  4 16:09 var -> usr/var
> 
> Both of them are directories (so it can't be a hard link).

What do you mean by "both"? On my system, /var is a soft link to
/usr/var, while /usr/var is (a hard link of) a directory.

> And, it would also mean that I can't deny anybody to erase the link that
> I made and substitute it with his/her link but just make the whole
> directory containing the link rewritable by the person responsible
> enough to not damage the link. It could be very dangerous indeed!

Someone who has write access to a directory can always remove entries
in this directory, regardless of type, ownership and permissons.
You don't want to have / writable for anyone but root, do you?

As a minor correction of my previous post, soft links are technically
files containing a path, so they have an inode of their own. However,
the permissions in this inode are not used, and DU won't create more 
than one hard link to a soft link:  after
  touch a
  ln -s a b
  ln b c
c will be another hard link for a. 
BTW, this is different e. g. on a Linux system: there, c will become a 
second hard link for the soft link b!

Regards,

Timo

