Nikola Milutinovic <Nikola.Milutinovic@ev.co.yu> writes:

>The question still stands. A soft link is a file. A file can be written
>to and I think I've seen some system calls that can manipulate either "a
>link to" and "a link itself". If you can't access the link itself, why
>would DEC put such access rights on one of the most common system soft
>links?

A soft/sym link is not a file.

It can be manipulated in only two ways:

	- unlink(2)		- remove it
	- symlink(2)		- create it

obviously, for both operations the permissions on the file are irrelevant
(the directory counts)

You could interpret permissions on the link, but typically (no
Unix implementation I know of) does so.

The links are displayed differently:

lrwxrwxrwx		SunOS 4/Solaris 2&7
l---------		Irix (others?)

Some may just use the user's umask() in creating them, (DU)?

Casper
