Sorry  Ernest,  I  sayed  that  input  filters  were  ignored  by remote
printers. But one can use filters also for  remote  printers,  the  only
trick  is  to set in the printcap the print device to /dev/null (a local
printer ;-) and forwards the output of  the  local  queue  to  a  remote
(second) printer queue. Thats the filter:

#!/bin/sh
(
echo \\033\E\\033\&l0O\\033\&l6D\\033\&l70P\\033\&l2E\\033\&l66F
echo \\033\&k2G\\033\(s12H\\033\&a12L\\033\&l0S\\033\(0N
cat -
echo \\033%-12345X
) | lpr -P remote_printer


BTW  The  question  has  been a uncompiled script as filter, if one uses
a2ps or the apsfilter packages everything is possible (also on dunix).

Juergen



