Sounds like what you need is a program called unixSex, written by one of
my colleagues. It will convert from time_t values (seconds from the
epoch) to and from a date string, allowing you to specify a format
string so the date can be in whatever format you want.

Since you don't have a compiler, I can mail you an executable if you
like.

For example:

titan:~$ unixSex -h
Usage:
   To print seconds since the Epoch (01/01/1970) to stdout:
      unixSex
   To print seconds since the Epoch for a date string to stdout (default
   fmt-string: "%Y%m%d%H%M%S"; use %b, %B, %d, %H, %m, %M, %S, %y or
%Y):
      unixSex "[date-str]" "[fmt-string]"
   The reverse: print time string (like output from 'date') to stdout:
      unixSex -v [seconds-since-Epoch]
   Same as the previous, allowing user-defined format (see 'man
strftime'):
      unixSex -v [seconds-since-Epoch] "[fmt-string]"
titan:~$ unixSex
912646323
titan:~$ unixSex -v 912646323
Thu Dec 03 10:52:03 AEST 1998

Or, more specifically to your question:

titan:~$ unixSex -v 912614795 "%d-%m-%Y %H:%M:%S"
03-12-1998 02:06:35
titan:~$ 

Heh... I guess we're in different timezones.

titan:~$ TZ=:GMT unixSex -v 912614795 "%d-%m-%Y %H:%M:%S"
02-12-1998 16:06:35
titan:~$

Boyd
--



