How are you shutting down the system?  You can't just reach for the Big
Red Switch (the power button).  A simple "shutdown now" isn't sufficient
either -- this takes you to single-user mode (it "shuts down" the system
for all other users), it doesn't halt the system.

You need to run "shutdown -h now" or "shutdown -r now" to fully halt the
system, and unmount all drives.  You should see console messages to the
effect that all drives are unmounted, and as a last message, remaining
filesystems are being mounted read-only.

The three-fingered salute <ctrl><alt><del> is coded to initiate system
shutdown in most Linux distros.

If this isn't the case, check back here with more details.  You might
also want to check under /etc/inittab and /etc/rc.d, particularly rc6.d.

-- 
